      "id": "network-defaults", "name": "Network defaults back", "tier": "collection",
      "summary": "Undoes network tweaks: auto-tuning, RSS, RSC, IPv6, the connectivity check.",
      "description": "Puts Windows' own network defaults back where a tweak tool changed them: TCP receive-window auto-tuning at Normal, receive-side scaling and receive segment coalescing on, IPv6 not turned off, and the internet connectivity check on. Each is documented by Microsoft and is Windows' default, so on a PC nobody tweaked nothing changes. Added in the 2026-10-24 quality pass after comparing network tweaks in a commercial tweak utility and the owner's corpus: none of their other network tweaks (Nagle, TCP timers and ports, MTU, adapter buffers and interrupts, BBRv2, QoS reserve) is documented, measured or safe enough for Mesura.",
      "operations": [ "manual.restore-tcp-autotuning", "repair.tcp-rss", "repair.tcp-rsc", "repair.ipv6-components", "repair.ncsi-active-probing" ]
    },
    {
      "id": "system-defaults", "name": "System defaults back", "tier": "collection",
      "summary": "Undoes tweak-pack changes to scheduling, memory and prefetch.",
      "description": "Puts the intended Windows values back where a tweak tool changed them: threaded DPCs on, the multimedia scheduler's reserve for background work at 20, the memory manager favouring programs over the file cache, and boot and app-launch prefetch on. Each undoes a tweak and is not a performance setting; none claims a frame-rate gain. On a PC nobody tweaked nothing changes. Not in Recommended on purpose: these are corrections for a PC a tweak tool changed, not settings every gaming PC needs, and none has a documented effect on games (the two Recommended repairs, threaded DPCs and the connectivity check, also appear here). Added in the 2026-10-25 audit follow-up to give these repairs a home beside the network ones.",
      "operations": [ "repair.threaded-dpc", "repair.mmcss-responsiveness", "repair.large-system-cache", "repair.prefetcher" ]
    },
    {
      "id": "less-data-sharing", "name": "Less data sharing", "tier": "collection",
      "summary": "Advertising ID, activity history, crash reports and other optional data sharing off.",
      "description": "Turns off optional data sharing with Microsoft and websites: optional diagnostic data, the advertising ID, tailored experiences, the language list sent to websites, app-launch tracking, typing and inking personalisation, online speech recognition, activity history, Windows Error Reporting and cross-device experiences. These are privacy choices, not speed-ups, and some are policies for the whole PC (Pro, Enterprise and Education). The first group overlaps Recommended and Maximum performance; this collection is the way to apply them without the rest. Windows keeps sending the diagnostic data it requires, and Edge, Microsoft 365 and other apps have settings of their own.",
      "operations": [ "privacy.diagnostic-data-required", "privacy.no-advertising-id", "privacy.no-tailored-experiences", "privacy.no-language-list", "privacy.no-app-launch-tracking", "privacy.no-typing-personalization", "privacy.no-online-speech", "privacy.no-activity-history", "privacy.no-error-reporting", "privacy.no-cross-device-experiences" ]
    },
    {
      "id": "ghost-spectre-superlite", "name": "Ghost Spectre Superlite follow-up", "tier": "specialized",
      "summary": "What a Ghost Spectre Superlite image still leaves to do.",
      "description": "What a Superlite image still leaves to do (checked against a 25H2 Superlite install, 2026-09-24): tips and suggestions and lock-screen tips (left on by the image), Start recommendations and recently opened files (not set by the image), setup nags and search highlights (not recorded at install), and the System Event Notification Service the image disables. Most of these stored values are community-documented; this is the owner's own follow-up list, read on that install. The image already hides Task View, the Copilot button and account prompts, shows file extensions, turns Game DVR off and removes OneDrive, so those aren't here. For a gaming PC, apply Recommended too. Preferences (search style, where Explorer opens, the classic right-click menu) are left to you.",
